#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "7fb3c0cb-147c-5d02-a470-c86591394f2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5192" :question/problem "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3$." :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1; y =-x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1 &= -x + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} &= -x + 2 \\\\\n1 &= (-x + 2)(x - 2) \\\\\n1 &= -x^{2} + 4x - 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (4 ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 - 20}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+3\n$$\n\ndoes not intersect the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/c1d0bf837c05cf29a2460425213cd5d1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "ffb2b712-52b9-5a09-9ac4-10a88f72f57e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5193" :question/problem "Find the points where the line $y=2x-3$ and the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}$ intersect (shown as the points $A$ and $B$ above).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/314995c0a1ee3ffb68009d42ba923120.png)" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = 2x - 3; y = \\frac{1}{x - 2}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3 &= \\frac{1}{x - 2} \\\\\n(x - 2)(2x - 3) &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 6 &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1, \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(1) - 3; =-1$,\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1)$.\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(\\frac{5}{2}) - 3; = 2$,\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"}] :question/id #uuid "b7b1d18b-256a-5aa4-9bcb-bd62e512231d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5260" :question/problem "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nintersects the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{k}{x + 3}; y = 2x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k}{x + 3} &= 2x + 3 \\\\\nk &= (2x + 3)(x + 3) \\\\\nk &= 2x^{2} + 9x + 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 9x + 9 - k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-9 ± \\sqrt{9^{2} - 4(2)(9 - k)})/(2(2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{81 - 72 + 8k}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{9 + 8k}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 + 8k & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < - 9 \\\\ k & < - \\frac{9}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is\n\n$$\nk<-\\dfrac{9}{8}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d34c6175-9688-5059-8fa8-1b0ffbdc2ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066696} {:answer/id #uuid "ec84c005-b0c4-5f24-8f20-1b900e2c2aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066697} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe6d6b7-a4dd-51af-929f-92316a34a023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066698} {:answer/id #uuid "374f6324-c4e9-594d-ac57-d6d7acd8d790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066699} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8e032d-580c-597d-a6a7-fecffef9e5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbe6d6b7-a4dd-51af-929f-92316a34a023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066698} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f44e5bc-fae7-5b9f-b732-de61d0f1c8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066695}] :question/id #uuid "a9bdc8c8-a628-5a28-9c53-49f974293c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-3471" :question/problem "The curve $y = \\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y - 5x = 8$ at the points $(p, q)$ and $(r, s)$. What is $p + q + r + s$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffa3959-bcae-53d4-b750-3acd046f4817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067068} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f13b0a-3767-5aac-9371-497ed9cc05f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067069} {:answer/id #uuid "15c4250c-ec22-5c0f-9869-16bf0a21f119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067070} {:answer/id #uuid "76e00e67-6b8c-5712-9e4a-778c86b82f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067071} {:answer/id #uuid "7c894e31-8bab-5c7b-bdc4-511f737f9090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76e00e67-6b8c-5712-9e4a-778c86b82f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067071} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ce676b-13f0-56a5-846b-a845f49e5220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067067}] :question/id #uuid "801b9317-386e-554f-9207-4d232469922d" :question/math-academy-id "q-41957" :question/problem "If the line $y =-2x + 3$ and the curve $y = \\frac{2}{x - 2} + 1$ intersect at the point $(p, q)$, then $p + q =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/re